WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.170
Film Courage: What are some of the best ways
to become a better actor aside from being

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:09.700
a perpetual student in some of these classes
that I know are beneficial but you’re right,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.070
people can get stuck in that, right?

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:15.400
Robin Riker, Actor/Author: And there is always
about classes, you want to be careful of teacher

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.910
as guru.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:23.810
You really have to be watchful for that because
there are many times where it’s more about

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:30.079
the teacher and the student’s adulation
of that teacher than it is about the students,

00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:31.189
you know?

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:39.840
So the way to become a better actor is to
act, is to get on stage as much as you possibly

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.960
can and don’t let any of this New York versus
Los Angeles theater thing dissuade you because

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:54.140
I’ve seen brilliant shows and stinkers in
big houses and small on both coasts.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.150
So you just have to do it.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.340
Actors act.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.490
If you sit around your house and get a play
reading together with other actors that you

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:06.310
know just read (and then you celebrate afterwards),
you do that.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:14.990
You have to do it because not and interacting
with other people is just social.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:26.259
It’s very important I think and being a
not so absorbed person because you learned

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:35.240
things from every experience being out in
the world watching the way people move listening

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:40.210
to the sounds of their voices and the ticks
they have and the things they do.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:47.749
It’s all about incorporating something real
into these fictional moments you are creating

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:53.640
on stage and the more reality you bring to
it the better you’ll be.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:01.409
Here is a tip I’ve found valuable I learned
this very early on because my mother was actually

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:07.469
my coach and when I was doing Shakespeare
festival for two seasons when I was a kid

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:11.670
and she was getting her Master’s there at
Boulder.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:17.319
So I had to audition for everything when she
wasn’t directing.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:24.989
And I liked language so I had a proclivity
for verse.

00:02:24.989 --> 00:02:32.739
But there were times when the verse…I mean
I was nine…so it didn’t fit in my mouth

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:33.739
quite right.

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:41.050
So what I learned was to put in my head…well
this doesn’t help with verse so much but

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:45.269
it helps with interpretation of anything and
in regular language this helps.

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:54.299
If there is a line that doesn’t fit in your
mouth well, rephrase that in your head the

00:02:54.299 --> 00:03:05.390
way you would say that concept and then impose
that rhythm onto the sentence that you’re

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.040
given.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.640
I wish I could have an example right now,
but if it’s something a simple as you know

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:20.140
“I want you to go to the store and buy some
celery” and that doesn’t make sense to

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.610
you, in your head you go “You know you need
celery?”

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.359
And then you go in your head “I want you
to go to the store and buy some celery.”

00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:33.430
So you say it with the music of the rewrite
that you put in your head.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:34.430
So you say it with the music of the rewrite
of the music that you put in your head, so

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:39.620
you impose the music of the rewrite on the
line that doesn’t fit in your mouth and

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.400
it really works!

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.299
Does that make sense to you?

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:44.569
Film Courage: Yeah, it sure does.

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:50.750
Because maybe it will be something that you
won’t normally say but you put it in your

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.750
voice.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.069
I can’t really articulate it but I get it.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:55.579
Robin: You put the music of your voice.

00:03:55.579 --> 00:03:57.599
You don’t put it in your words.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:02.989
You put it in the music that accompanies your
words.

00:04:02.989 --> 00:04:05.930
So your superimposing the sound of “You
know?

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.140
We could use some celery.”

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.989
On “I want you to go to the store and get
some celery.”

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:13.400
Instead of “I want you to go to the store
and get some celery” (lowers her voice).

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:21.620
Or whatever, that’s a very silly example
but it’s the concept of the tone imposed

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:28.789
on the words that don’t fit in your mouth,
make the concept fit in your head and then

00:04:28.789 --> 00:04:32.170
put the music of that over your words that
don’t fit.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:36.960
Film Courage: Right, so if you say things
with maybe an inflection at the end, a question

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:44.690
or something or maybe it’s more of a directive,
whatever way you normally speak to someone

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:49.819
in that context you do it and try to fit that
sentence in?

00:04:49.819 --> 00:04:54.889
Robin: The sound of that sentence over the
words that don’t fit because every actor

00:04:54.889 --> 00:05:00.470
has gotten a line that “I would never say
this?”

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:01.840
And “Can’t make this real.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.480
How do I make this real?”

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:14.069
So you reinterpret that sentence to your own
words and hear how that sounds in your head

00:05:14.069 --> 00:05:18.330
and put the sound of that on the words that
don’t fit.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:24.849
And it’s always worked for me and when I’ve
given it as advice to others they’ve said

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:28.410
“I like that, that really helps.”

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:34.479
Because it personalizes it, it makes it real
listening to the tones of others and that

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:35.479
sort of thing.

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:36.479
It’s very helpful.

00:05:36.479 --> 00:05:38.250
So listen to your own tones too and have them
help you.

